the Linux documentation is fairly terse - from man execve:

       execve() executes the  program  pointed  to  by  filename.
       filename  must  be either a binary executable, or a script
       starting with a line of the form "#!  interpreter  [arg]".
       In  the latter case, the interpreter must be a valid path-
       name for an executable which is not itself a script, which
       will be invoked as interpreter [arg] filename.

The HP-UX definition is longer (man exec):

     For a script file, the initial line of a script file must begin with
     #! as the first two bytes, followed by zero or more spaces, followed
     by interpreter or interpreter argument, as in:

          #! interpreter [argument]

     One or more spaces or tabs must separate interpreter and argument.
     The first line should end with either a newline or a null character.

     When the script file is executed, the system executes the specified
     interpreter as an executable object file.  Even in the case of
     execlp() or execvp(), no path searching is done of the interpreter
     name.

     The argument is anything that follows interpreter and tabs or spaces.
     If an argument is given, it is passed to the interpreter as argv[1],
     and the name of the script file is passed as argv[2].  Otherwise, the
     name of the script file is passed as argv[1].  argv[0] is passed as
     specified in the exec*() call.  All other arguments specified in the
     exec*() call are passed following the name of the script file (that
     is, beginning at argv[3] if there is an argument; otherwise, at
     argv[2]).

     Some interpreters process the interpreter and the argument internally,
     and do not provide the interpreter and the argument to the users
     script.

     If the initial line of the script file exceeds a system-defined
     maximum number of characters, exec*() fails.  The minimum value for
     this limit is 32.

These are based on the SVID definitions of exec.

Given the two scripts:

      $ cat awk1
      #!/usr/bin/gawk -f
      BEGIN {
      print "awk1 entered"
      }
      $ cat awk2
      #!/usr/bin/gawk -f /tmp/awk1 -f
      BEGIN {
      print "awk2 entered"
      }
      $ ./awk2
      awk2: fatal: can't open source file " /tmp/awk1 -f"
      for reading (No such file or directory)

In this case, gawk received as argv's

   argv[1] = "-f /tmp/awk1 -f"

getopts parsed out the -f and treated the rest of the text as a
contiguous OPTARG - so the OPTARG was " /tmp/awk1 -f" - note the leading
space.

BSD derived systems might treat this differently,  but certainly
not HP-UX which is SVID conformant.  Might I suggest a little
experimentation?


I will once again post my awkfe program (I need to LGPL this sucker
someday) that allows for includes.  First the man page:

======================================================================
Name
     awkfe - front end for awk

Synopsis
     awkfe [-h] [awk options] files ...

Description
     AWK is a program used to filter text using regular expression pattern
     matching.  AWK scripts can be made directly executable with the use of
     an exec line like:

          #!/usr/bin/awk -f

     Exec examines the first two bytes of an executable file for it's magic
     number. The special magic number '#!' instructs exec to parse the
     first line for the full path name of a shell executable and up to 1
     additional option to pass.  Exec then calls the shell executable
     passing it the option,  if there is one, and the name of the script
     file.  Thus,  the argument array passed to /usr/bin/awk of a file
     named /usr/local/bin/tstawk (where /usr/local/bin is in the PATH),
     invoked as:

          tstawk -F: db_data

     with a first line of:

          #!/usr/bin/awk -f

     would be:

          argv[0] = "/usr/local/bin/tstawk"
          argv[1] = "-f"
          argv[2] = "/usr/local/bin/tstawk"
          argv[3] = " -F:"
          argv[4] = "db_data"

     AWK supports code reusability with the "-f" option.  You can include
     multiple awk scripts by using multiple "-f" options.  However,  if you
     try that within a self-contained script,  for instance using:

          #!/usr/bin/awk -f awk_db_funs -f

     then the array passed to awk is:

          argv[0] = "/usr/local/bin/tstawk"
          argv[1] = "-f"
          argv[2] = "awk_db_funs -f"
          argv[3] = "/usr/local/bin/tstawk"
          argv[4] = " -F:"
          argv[5] = "db_data"

     and AWK will complain that it can't open file "awk_db_funs -f" . In
     addition, exec truncates the parsed line to 32 characters beginning
     with the original "/".

     Awkfe was written to allow code reuse with a simple,  familiar syntax.
     You insert an exec tag line pointing at the awkfe executable prior to
     your original AWK tag line:

          #!/usr/local/bin/awkfe
          #!/usr/bin/awk -f

     Then to include resuable code segments, you use #include statements.
     For instance:

          #!/usr/local/bin/awkfe
          #!/usr/bin/awk -f
          #include <db_data>

     The second tag line is only required if you want to point at a
     different awk ,  for instance at GNU awk (gawk):

          #!/usr/local/bin/awkfe
          #!/usr/local/bin/gawk

     The second tag line does not require the -f option since that is
     ignored anyway.

Note!
     Awkfe ceases to scan for #include lines after the first non-commented
     line.  So placing a blank line after your #include lines will result
     in faster processing.

External Influences
     There are two different #include formats that are supported:

          #include <db_data>

     and

          #include "/usr/local/awk/db_data"

     The second format merely passes the literal string enclosed in double
     quotes to awk. With the first format, awkfe extracts the file name and
     attempts to resolve the name to a file name using the AWKPATH
     environment variable by opening for read any filename constructed from
     elements of the AWKPATH and the #include name.  If no AWKPATH variable
     exists, awkfe constructs one from the base directory of the executable
     script, the "." directory and the user's HOME directory.

Diagnostics
     Awkfe returns error messages if it can't resolve a #include <db_data>
     filename,  or for a poorly constructed #include line or memory
     problems.  All other diagnostics are generated by AWK.

Return Value
     For errors noted above, awkfe will return either a 1 or a 2.  All
     other errors are returned by awk.

Examples
     #!/usr/local/bin/awkfe
     #!/usr/local/bin/gawk
     #include <rddesc>
     #include "/usr/local/awk/printload"

     BEGIN { ...

See Also
     awk(1)
======================================================================



